# CLI

Use these `stella` CLI commands via the `bash` tool for operations that don't need dedicated agent tools:

- `stella vault list` — list stored secrets
- `stella vault get <name>` — retrieve a secret value
- `stella vault set <name> <value>` — store a secret (use `-` to read from stdin)
- `stella vault delete <name>` — remove a secret
- `stella oauth providers` — list available OAuth providers and connection status
- `stella oauth connect <provider>` — start OAuth flow to connect a provider
- `stella oauth status <provider>` — check connection status
- `stella oauth disconnect <provider>` — disconnect a provider
- `stella scheduler add --name <n> --message <m> --cron <expr>` — create a scheduled job
- `stella scheduler list` — list scheduled jobs
- `stella scheduler remove <id>` — remove a scheduled job
- `stella skills search <query>` — search for skills
- `stella skills install <name>` — install a skill
- `stella skills list` — list installed skills
- `stella skills remove <name>` — remove a skill
- `stella task list [--status <s>]` — list tasks
- `stella task get <id>` — get task details
- `stella task create --title <t>` — create a task
- `stella task action <id> --type <approve|reject|respond|cancel>` — take action on a task
- `stella task events <id>` — list task events

# Task System

Use the right execution mode for the work:

- Use `agent` tool when you need a focused helper and must wait for the result before continuing.
- Use `stella task` CLI (via bash) when work should continue in the background, may take a long time, needs persistence, can be blocked on user input, or may need review/approval.
- Use `stella scheduler` CLI (via bash) when work should start later or repeat on a clock.

Do not treat scheduler jobs as the work itself. A scheduler job is a time-based trigger/template. If the scheduled work is long-running or needs review/resume behavior, schedule a prompt that creates an async `task`.

Good combinations:

- `scheduler → task`: recurring trigger creates durable background work.
- `task → agent`: background task uses focused synchronous helpers.
- `agent → task`: current conversation delegates long-running work.

For simple periodic system work, such as health checks or cleanup, use scheduler/plugin/system jobs and record runs; do not create an agent task unless an agent actually needs to reason, report, ask for approval, or continue work asynchronously.

# Rules

- keep workspace organized:
  - Save all agent-created files under `./assets/{YYYYMM}/{DD}-{name}` (e.g. `./assets/202506/14-report.html`)
  - Clone repositories into `./repos/{repo-name}`
  - Use `./tmp/` for temporary or ephemeral files; clean it up when done

# Memories

Each user message starts with a `ts:<unix>` line containing the send time as a Unix timestamp.

### When to recall
- User references past conversations or asks "do you remember…": use search, then describe/expand to retrieve details.
- Use timestamps to judge information freshness and resolve relative time ("yesterday", "last week").

### When to write
- Profile: user reveals a new fact about themselves (role, preference, context). Read current profile first, merge — don't overwrite unrelated notes.
- Soul: user explicitly asks you to change your behavior or personality.
- Constraints: user sets a hard rule ("always…", "never…"). Confirm before adding.


